------------------------------------- HTTP with authentication ---------------------------------------


- SOME REST ENDPOINTS ARE SECURED: LET'S SEE WHAT WE NEED TO DO TO CALL THEM, AND HOW TO USE
    INTERCEPTORS. ALSO A LITTLE INTRODUCTION TO JSON WEB TOKEN AND TO HOW TO USE ENVIRONMENT FILES.


- Now that the user is able to log in (and out), we should be able to use the APIs of PonyRacer
    that require to be authenticated.
    The mechanism used is JWT (JSON Web Token). If you don’t know anything about JWT, you can browse
    https://www.jwt.io/.

    The general principle of this mechanism is quite simple:
        👉 The user logs in to a server and gets back a JSON token;
        👉 this token contains a clear-text payload containing information that the application needs
            to know about the user;
        👉 this token is cryptographically signed with a secret key that only the server knows
            (which means that you can’t counterfeit a token without knowing this secret key);
        👉 this token is sent with every HTTP request to the server, in the Authorization header.
        👉 the server checks the authenticity of the token, extracts the information it needs from
            the token, and responds to the request.

- We have already obtained this token when authenticating: it’s one of the fields of the object
    returned after a successful login.

- All we need to do now is to make sure this token is sent with every HTTP request the application
    sends.

- 🔥Since we need the same header to be sent for every request, it is the perfect use case for an HTTP
    interceptor.


1️⃣ But let’s start with a bit of refactoring. Hardcoding the URL of the API every time we use it
    does not feel right: what if we want to use another URL when we are in dev, staging or
    production? Let’s use the "replacement file" feature of the CLI to add environment properties
    to our application.
    💡A CLI schematic is going to create for us a folder environments in src with two files
    environment.ts and environment.development.ts:

        ng generate environments



2️⃣ Then we can replace the content of each file with:

    environment.development.ts:

        export const environment = {
            baseUrl: 'https://ponyracer.ninja-squad.com'
        };


    environment.ts:

        export const environment = {
            baseUrl: 'https://ponyracer.ninja-squad.com'
        };



3️⃣ Notice that the angular.json file as been modified by the schematic as well to add the
    following option to the build configuration:

        "fileReplacements": [
            {
                "replace": "src/environments/environment.ts",
                "with": "src/environments/environment.development.ts"
            }
        ]



4️⃣ Angular CLI will then use the "development" file or the "production" file, depending on which
    environment you specify 🔥when you build/serve the app, even if as a developer, you always import
    from the environment.ts file. For example, 
    🔥ng serve --configuration=production will use the constant from the environment.ts file,
    🔥whereas ng serve uses the constant from the environment.development.ts file.


5️⃣ Import the environment constant from the environment.ts file in user-service.ts, and refactor the
    hardcoded URLs by using the environment.baseUrl value. Do the same in RaceService.


6️⃣ Now that this refactoring is done, we just need to add the JWT feature. ⚠️We could pass the secret
    token every time we do an HTTP request, but that would be cumbersome. The HttpClient provided by
    Angular has a useful feature though: you can define interceptors that will be called for every
    request.
    First, create an interceptor called jwtInterceptor. The CLI offers a generator to do so:

        ng generate interceptor jwt
    
    ⚠️This interceptor does nothing yet: it just gives the request to the next interceptor in the
    chain.


7️⃣ Now, in the function of our interceptor, let’s get the current user (you need to inject UserService
    in the interceptor thanks to inject()). If we have a user, we should add a header to the request
    before giving it to the next handler. The new header must be named Authorization and its value
    must be Bearer ${user.token}. For example, if the token value is 'secret', the header value must
    be 'Bearer secret'. To set a header in an intercepted request, you must use the clone method of
    the request, which takes an optional parameter. This parameter has a field setHeaders, to which
    you can give the header you want to set with an object literal, like { 'hello': 'world' }. You
    should then give the cloned request to the next handler. If there is no token just give the
    original request to the next handler.


8️⃣ We now need to register our interceptor in our application: 
    use provideHttpClient(withInterceptors([myInterceptor])) in the providers of appConfig.


